    "body": "## Problem\n\n`LOOP_PROCESS_EVENTS_UNTIL` subtracts the whole elapsed milliseconds from the remaining time, then resets its start time to \"now\", so the sub-millisecond remainder is lost. If every pass returns in under 1 ms (for example because the condition closes a libuv handle, which makes the next poll return at once), the remaining time never goes down and the loop never times out.\n\n`vim.wait()` on master no longer uses this macro (it tracks an absolute deadline in Lua), but Vimscript `wait()` still hangs on master:\n\n```vim\necho wait(500, 'luaeval(\"(function() vim.uv.new_timer():close() return false end)()\")')\n```\n\nOn 0.11/0.12, `vim.wait()` goes through the same macro, which is the hang reported in #42538.\n\n## Solution\n\nAdvance the start time only by the whole milliseconds that were counted.\n\nFixes #42538\n\nTests: new case in `test/functional/vimscript/wait_spec.lua`. It hangs on master and passes with the fix. `wait_spec.lua` (8), `core/job_spec.lua` (66) and `lua/vim_spec.lua` (130) pass; stylua and uncrustify are clean.\n\nAI-assisted; I reviewed the change and ran the tests above.\n",
    "closed_at": "2026-10-30T11:44:17Z",
